ssh-vpn-gui (0.2.42) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Send the long SSH tunnel inside TLS, so the connection looks like
    ordinary data transfer instead of SSH. A short plain SSH session
    still starts a forwarder on the server (port 443, or 8443 or 2053
    if 443 is taken). The tunnel client pins that certificate and
    refuses a different one. An HTTP probe gets a short file response;
    only an SSH banner is forwarded to sshd. The server needs python3,
    openssl, and the chosen port open in the provider firewall.

 -- SSH VPN GUI Maintainers <maintainers@example.invalid>  Fri, 30 Oct 2026 11:52:11 +0300

ssh-vpn-gui (0.2.41) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Keep interactive-looking traffic on the SSH session that carries the
    tunnel. The server used to sleep silently after the tunnel came up,
    so the connection looked like a pure bulk tunnel. Now the client
    sends a short line every 5 to 10 seconds and the server discards it
    and replies with one cheap command (date, uptime, load, id, uname,
    whoami, df of /, or hostname). Nothing walks the disk, and the line
    from the client is never executed. This only reshapes an otherwise
    quiet tunnel; it does not hide a tunnel under heavy traffic.

